"05d28970-6cfe-5fc6-bc98-6f13f1fbcff5" :question/math-academy-id "q-39270" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{2} \\\\ 7{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{2}=0 \\\\ 7{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n7{c}_{1}-0=14\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5eb02b51-88b6-54c6-8e3a-87a5f60dbdfd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5eb02b51-88b6-54c6-8e3a-87a5f60dbdfd" :answer-field/id #uuid "5eb02b51-88b6-54c6-8e3a-87a5f60dbdfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fde1093c-8ee3-592c-af49-4b7e84a1917a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e645af68-19cd-5af5-9c27-6263465a9288" "answer-fde1093c-8ee3-592c-af49-4b7e84a1917a" "answer-2867dfa3-059a-58fa-b11f-d0bedd4e7491" "answer-24ed8036-d2f0-5678-aded-6e9c044ca85d" "answer-9dd33a37-99bf-5651-bb5f-06e9f817bff2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e645af68-19cd-5af5-9c27-6263465a9288" :answer/id #uuid "e645af68-19cd-5af5-9c27-6263465a9288" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fde1093c-8ee3-592c-af49-4b7e84a1917a" :answer/id #uuid "fde1093c-8ee3-592c-af49-4b7e84a1917a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2867dfa3-059a-58fa-b11f-d0bedd4e7491" :answer/id #uuid "2867dfa3-059a-58fa-b11f-d0bedd4e7491" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24ed8036-d2f0-5678-aded-6e9c044ca85d" :answer/id #uuid "24ed8036-d2f0-5678-aded-6e9c044ca85d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9dd33a37-99bf-5651-bb5f-06e9f817bff2" :answer/id #uuid "9dd33a37-99bf-5651-bb5f-06e9f817bff2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c7ea76f4-22db-56a5-acbc-fee92d706737" :question/id #uuid "c7ea76f4-22db-56a5-acbc-fee92d706737" :question/math-academy-id "q-79729" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $x(t),$ and that $x(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nfind the position of the particle at $t=3$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=3$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n-{c}_{2}=6\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+(-6)=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=10.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =10\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+(-6)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $x(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3) & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\cdot 3}+[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{9}+[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10{e}^{9}+12{e}^{-6} \\\\ 10{e}^{9}-6{e}^{-6}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f4863853-4c96-5cb6-a514-351184df6735"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f4863853-4c96-5cb6-a514-351184df6735" :answer-field/id #uuid "f4863853-4c96-5cb6-a514-351184df6735" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-579b934f-3562-585a-9c58-868d0ac299b3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fbd36ee8-9de1-5381-988c-ad17c88ac46f" "answer-579b934f-3562-585a-9c58-868d0ac299b3" "answer-161a2113-6f77-577f-b8dd-070e7d29117d" "answer-9ec3aa86-7c65-5073-99dc-9b9434d81635" "answer-bcad915f-ea19-5199-a4f9-a7af8a70c018"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbd36ee8-9de1-5381-988c-ad17c88ac46f" :answer/id #uuid "fbd36ee8-9de1-5381-988c-ad17c88ac46f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10{e}^{9} \\\\ 5{e}^{9}+6{e}^{-6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-579b934f-3562-585a-9c58-868d0ac299b3" :answer/id #uuid "579b934f-3562-585a-9c58-868d0ac299b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10{e}^{9}+12{e}^{-6} \\\\ 10{e}^{9}-6{e}^{-6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-161a2113-6f77-577f-b8dd-070e7d29117d" :answer/id #uuid "161a2113-6f77-577f-b8dd-070e7d29117d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10{e}^{9}-4{e}^{-6} \\\\ -10{e}^{9}+2{e}^{-6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ec3aa86-7c65-5073-99dc-9b9434d81635" :answer/id #uuid "9ec3aa86-7c65-5073-99dc-9b9434d81635" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{9}+{e}^{-6} \\\\ -{e}^{9}-{e}^{-6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcad915f-ea19-5199-a4f9-a7af8a70c018" :answer/id #uuid "bcad915f-ea19-5199-a4f9-a7af8a70c018" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}512{e}^{-6} \\\\ 5{e}^{9}-2{e}^{-6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5cca3049-b571-5225-b8ec-fa2863b439e4" :question/id #uuid "5cca3049-b571-5225-b8ec-fa2863b439e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-327159" :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned},\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=1$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]e & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+3e{c}_{2} \\\\ {e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+3e{c}_{2}=5{e}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e{c}_{2}=3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4e{c}_{2}=8{e}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{2}=2e$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e(2e)=3{e}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}={e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{-3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+2e[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e8b9b574-5653-5108-921b-66096e71af6e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e8b9b574-5653-5108-921b-66096e71af6e" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8b9b574-5653-5108-921b-66096e71af6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b2b7d681-5156-5af4-87b3-2921a9b8733f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-27a45e59-f7b3-564a-a272-ca90d193285e" "answer-da8ce87a-813f-5c97-a095-3e683c139a0f" "answer-eb72e68d-812c-59e1-a023-f042eb2f9be0" "answer-b2b7d681-5156-5af4-87b3-2921a9b8733f" "answer-eca08a37-57cc-5960-814d-b7ab701d6768"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27a45e59-f7b3-564a-a272-ca90d193285e" :answer/id #uuid "27a45e59-f7b3-564a-a272-ca90d193285e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)=2{e}^{-3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+2[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da8ce87a-813f-5c97-a095-3e683c139a0f" :answer/id #uuid "da8ce87a-813f-5c97-a095-3e683c139a0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{-2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+2[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb72e68d-812c-59e1-a023-f042eb2f9be0" :answer/id #uuid "eb72e68d-812c-59e1-a023-f042eb2f9be0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{-3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-5t}+2e[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2b7d681-5156-5af4-87b3-2921a9b8733f" :answer/id #uuid "b2b7d681-5156-5af4-87b3-2921a9b8733f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{-3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+2e[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eca08a37-57cc-5960-814d-b7ab701d6768" :answer/id #uuid "eca08a37-57cc-5960-814d-b7ab701d6768" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-5t}+2e[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130360 :tutorial/content "An **initial value problem (IVP)** for a homogeneous linear system has the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),\n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant $2 \\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf{x}_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is the given initial state at $t=t_0.$ In other words, in an IVP we seek the solution of $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ that passes through the point $\\mathbf{x}_0$ at $t=t_0.$\n\nTo solve an IVP, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Find the general solution of the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1\\mathbf{x}_1(t)+c_2\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\n- Substitute $t=t_0$ into the general solution and use the initial condition $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=c_1\\mathbf{x}_1(t_0)+c_2\\mathbf{x}_2(t_0)\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\nc_1\\mathbf{x}_1(t_0)+c_2\\mathbf{x}_2(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}_0\n$$\n\n- Solve the resulting linear system for $c_1$ and $c_2,$ then substitute those values back into $\\mathbf{x}(t).$\n\nThe definition (and the procedure) above generalizes directly to the $n\\times n$ case.\n\nLet's see some concrete examples of solving an IVP." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129399 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
